 ## Background
-DASNeRF was proposed in a paper published in *PLOS ONE* in 2026 (based on the source date) [S1]. It builds upon the original NeRF framework [S1] and incorporates techniques from Mip-NeRF (cone frustum sampling) [S1]. The method addresses the limitation of NeRF requiring hundreds of images by leveraging monocular depth estimation (specifically DepthAnythingV2) to guide learning in sparse input scenarios [S1]. It was developed to improve sparse reconstruction details and reduce the impact of lighting on detail reconstruction [S1].
+DASNeRF was proposed in a paper published in *PLOS ONE* in 2024/2025 (DOI: 10.1371/journal.pone.0321878) [S1]. It builds on the original NeRF framework [S1] and incorporates techniques from Mip-NeRF for cone frustum sampling [S1]. The method addresses the limitation of NeRF requiring hundreds of images by introducing depth consistency optimization and adaptive sampling for sparse views [S1]. It specifically selects DepthAnythingV2 over MiDaS for its superior performance on complex geometries and speed [S1].
 
